# TestFlow Frontend Rules

## Technology

The TestFlow frontend uses:
- Next.js
- React
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS

Do not introduce another frontend framework or CSS/UI styling library without explicit permission.

## Styling

Use Tailwind CSS for application styling.

Do not introduce:
- Bootstrap
- Material UI
- Chakra UI
- styled-components
- Emotion
- Sass/SCSS
- another CSS framework or component styling library

unless explicitly approved.

If existing shared TestFlow UI components are available, reuse them instead of recreating equivalent components.

## UI Consistency

Maintain visual consistency with the existing TestFlow application.

Before creating or modifying UI:
1. Inspect similar existing screens/components.
2. Reuse existing spacing, typography, border radius, form styles, cards, buttons, badges, modals, and layout patterns.
3. Reuse shared components whenever possible.
4. Do not redesign unrelated parts of a page.

A new feature should look like part of the existing TestFlow application, not like a separate design system.

## Components

Prefer existing shared components over page-specific duplicates.

Do not create a new Button, Input, Select, Modal, Badge, Card, or similar primitive if an appropriate shared component already exists.

Keep Storybook-compatible shared components consistent with their existing APIs.

## Scope

When implementing a frontend feature:
- change only the required UI
- preserve existing responsive behavior
- preserve existing navigation and workflows
- do not add new screens, dialogs, animations, or interactions unless requested

If the requirement would require changing the existing design system or shared component API, ask before doing so.